from pathlib import Path
BASE=Path(__file__).parent

def viewer(condition, title, caption):
    return f'''<figure class="case-viewer" data-case="{condition}"><div class="figure-head"><div><span class="figure-kicker">SAME RECORDED ACTIONS · SAVED MODEL OUTPUTS</span><h3>{title}</h3></div><label>Example<select data-scene aria-label="Example for {title}"><option value="0">1 of 3</option><option value="1">2 of 3</option><option value="2">3 of 3</option></select></label></div><div class="case-frames"><div class="case-frame real"><p><span class="dot"></span>Ground truth</p><canvas data-view="gt" width="63" height="63" role="img" aria-label="Recorded real observation"></canvas></div><div class="case-frame real-encoding"><p><span class="dot hollow"></span>JEPA · real latent</p><div class="canvas-wrap"><canvas data-view="real_encoded" width="63" height="63" role="img" aria-label="Decoded real JEPA latent"></canvas><span class="frame-pending" data-real-pending>Real-future decode<br>export pending</span></div></div><div class="case-frame jepa"><p><span class="dot"></span>JEPA imagination</p><canvas data-view="jepa" width="63" height="63" role="img" aria-label="Decoded JEPA predicted latent"></canvas></div><div class="case-frame flow"><p><span class="dot"></span>Flow sample</p><canvas data-view="flow" width="63" height="63" role="img" aria-label="Decoded flow sample"></canvas></div></div><div class="case-scrubber"><button type="button" data-play>Play</button><button type="button" data-observe>Observed frame</button><label><span data-time-label>Future</span> <output data-time>+4</output></label><input data-time-slider type="range" min="0" max="16" step="1" value="4" aria-label="Frame time for {title}"></div><p class="case-status" data-status aria-live="polite">Loading saved frames…</p><div class="recorded-actions" data-actions aria-label="Recorded action sequence" hidden></div><details class="flow-reconstruction" data-flow-reconstruction hidden><summary>Inspect the flow reconstruction of the same real frame</summary><div><canvas data-view="flow_encoded" width="63" height="63" role="img" aria-label="Flow reconstruction of the real frame"></canvas><p>This view decodes a real observation through the flow tokenizer. It is separate from the sampled future above.</p></div></details><figcaption>{caption} <span data-selection>Historical windows were selected by total cube travel, before comparing model outputs. Fresh fixed-index exports are pending.</span></figcaption></figure>'''

html='''<!doctype html><html lang="en"><head><meta charset="utf-8"><meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1"><title>What should a world model learn to ignore?</title><meta name="description" content="A visual study of useful abstraction, failed state readout and the limits of mean-based stochastic planning."><link rel="icon" href="data:image/svg+xml,%3Csvg xmlns=%27http://www.w3.org/2000/svg%27 viewBox=%270 0 32 32%27%3E%3Crect width=%2732%27 height=%2732%27 fill=%27%2317324d%27/%3E%3Crect x=%2710%27 y=%2710%27 width=%2712%27 height=%2712%27 fill=%27%23cd663e%27/%3E%3C/svg%3E"><link rel="stylesheet" href="styles.css"><link rel="stylesheet" href="redcube.css"><script defer src="assets/historical/historical-story.js"></script><script defer src="assets/fresh-story.js"></script><script defer src="article.js"></script></head><body><a class="skip" href="#main">Skip to article</a><header class="masthead"><a class="wordmark" href="#main">WORLD MODELS <span>/</span> THE RED CUBE</a><nav aria-label="Article"><a href="#works">Useful abstraction</a><a href="#state">State readout</a><a href="#planning">Planning with a mean</a></nav><span class="edition">RESEARCH COMPANION</span></header><main id="main"><div class="draft-banner" data-internal-note><strong>Internal draft.</strong> The H3/H1 primary and distance-response evaluations are complete. The shared-CNN comparison for the stochastic-control case is pending. Case 3 is currently an analytical result, not an established empirical mechanism.</div><section class="hero redcube-hero"><p class="eyebrow">ONE SMALL WORLD. TWO DIFFERENT FAILURE MODES.</p><h1>What should a world model<br><em>learn to ignore?</em></h1><p class="dek">Red-cube experiments examine what a learned representation keeps. Two exact analytical examples show a separate limitation: a perfect mean prediction can still give the wrong stochastic decision.</p><p class="hero-intro">Predicting every pixel wastes effort on visual noise. Predicting an embedding can avoid that cost. But the embedding must still keep the cube, and its imagined future must preserve what a decision depends on. These are separate requirements.</p><div class="world-legend"><span><i class="cube-icon" aria-hidden="true"></i>One controlled cube</span><span>Four directional actions</span><span>Changing or persistent visual noise</span><span>Still dynamics or hidden wind</span></div></section>
<section class="article-section case-section" id="works"><div class="section-label">01 / STATE IS READABLE; CONTROL IS A HARDER TEST</div><div class="prose"><h2>It can reach nearby goals.<br>The range is short.</h2><p>Start with deterministic motion and a gray background redrawn every frame. The background is unpredictable; the action-driven motion is not. Here, the grid JEPA retains the cube’s position while its decoder removes much of the visual noise.</p><p>The saved forecasts below follow the same action sequence. The model’s simplified image can be useful even though it does not reproduce the background.</p></div>'''
html+=viewer('gray_frame_still','Fresh visual noise. Deterministic motion.','The observation, real-latent reconstruction and imagined future answer different questions. At time 0, the two model columns reconstruct the observed context. Later times show autoregressive forecasts; a flow sample need not reproduce the recorded noise.')
html+='''<p class="result-line"><strong>0.12 px</strong><span>average position-readout L₁ error on real latents, across three grid-JEPA fits.</span><a href="#methods">Measurement details ↓</a></p><div class="prose"><p>The representation preserves state, and these short forecasts keep the cube visible. A requested visual goal gives a harder test: can the agent turn that representation into useful movement?</p><h3>Three-step search supports nearby goal control.</h3><p>With the model unchanged, a three-step search reaches every one- and two-move goal in all three model fits. At three moves, it reaches <strong>121–124 of 128 goals</strong>; one-step search reaches only 16–22. The search depth therefore matters, even in this small arena. The planner searches native action sequences, sums their predicted latent distances to the goal image, executes one action, and replans.</p><p>The same controller reaches only 25–34 goals at six moves, 1–3 at nine moves, and none at twelve. Every distance uses the same 128 starting histories and a 24-action limit. This is a bounded result about nearby goal-directed control.</p><p>This operating-range study was designed after an unsuccessful 6–12-move evaluation. It keeps the same three-step controller and reports all six distance shells. The original failure remains part of the record below.</p></div><figure class="goal-range-figure"><div class="figure-head"><div><span class="figure-kicker">POST-OUTCOME STUDY · SAME CONTROLLER · ALL SIX DISTANCES</span><h3>Three-step search has a short operating range.</h3></div><div class="range-view-switch" role="group" aria-label="View performance or actual paths"><button type="button" data-range-view="performance" aria-pressed="true">Operating range</button><button type="button" data-range-view="paths" aria-pressed="false">Actual paths</button></div></div><label class="range-seed-control" data-range-seed-control hidden>World-model seed<select id="range-model-seed"><option value="0">0</option><option value="1">1</option><option value="2">2</option></select></label><div class="goal-path-scroll" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="Goal-control performance across all distances and model seeds"><img id="range-main-image" src="assets/goal-distance/goal_distance_response.svg" alt="Complete operating-range curve for model seeds zero, one and two. H3 reaches all one- and two-move goals, nearly all three-move goals, and sharply fewer goals at six and nine moves; none at twelve. H1 is substantially worse at two and three moves. Every distance and model is shown."></div><p class="goal-swipe">On a narrow screen, scroll to inspect every model and distance.</p><figcaption id="range-caption">The unchanged H3 controller reaches nearby goals reliably, then loses success with distance. Every model seed and distance is retained. Error bars are Wilson intervals for 128 starts separately in each cell; the same prefixes are reused across shells. The state reference is privileged. This diagnostic was designed after the original hard-goal failure.</figcaption><details class="range-rgb-details"><summary>Inspect the unmodified RGB executions for fixed case 0</summary><div><p>Case 0 was fixed within every distance shell before the follow-up outcomes. These are actual simulator executions, ending at the first hit, death, or 24-action limit.</p><label>World-model seed<select id="range-rgb-seed"><option value="0">0</option><option value="1">1</option><option value="2">2</option></select></label><div class="goal-path-scroll" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="Actual RGB executions across the six goal distances"><img id="range-rgb-image" src="assets/goal-distance/goal_distance_rgb_m0.svg" alt="Unmodified actual JEPA H3 execution frames for fixed case zero at distances one, two, three, six, nine and twelve, model seed zero."></div></div></details><p class="range-source"><a href="assets/goal-distance/counts_by_distance.json">All exact counts</a> · <a href="assets/goal-distance/figure_manifest.json">Frame, action and model provenance</a> · <a href="assets/goals/distance-audit.json">Independent audit</a></p></figure><details class="quiet-details original-goal-study"><summary>Original 6–12-move test: H3 reached only 5, 5 and 9 of 128 goals</summary><div><p>The original test remains unchanged: H3 hits were 5, 5 and 9 of 128 across the three model checkpoints; H1 reached none, random actions reached 3, and a privileged state reference reached all 128. This unsuccessful result motivated the distance-response follow-up. Both use a 24-action limit, but the follow-up changes the goal-distance distribution.</p><figure class="goal-control-figure"><div class="figure-head"><div><span class="figure-kicker">REAL EXECUTION · DETERMINISTIC DYNAMICS · 24-ACTION LIMIT</span><h3>The fixed examples show the failure.</h3></div><label>World-model seed<select id="goal-model-seed"><option value="0">0 · 5 / 128 goals</option><option value="1">1 · 5 / 128 goals</option><option value="2">2 · 9 / 128 goals</option></select></label></div><div class="goal-path-scroll" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="Actual paths for the three predeclared goal-control episodes"><img id="goal-path-image" src="assets/goals/goal_paths_checkpoint0.svg" alt="Actual trajectories for predeclared episodes zero, one and two using model seed zero. The three-step JEPA planner fails to reach each goal; the privileged state reference reaches them."></div><p class="goal-swipe">On a narrow screen, scroll the figure to inspect all three fixed episodes.</p><figcaption>Episodes 0, 1 and 2 were fixed before outcomes. The traces show actual simulator positions on a faded initial observation; coordinates are annotations, not JEPA inputs. The state reference uses privileged position information. All policies share the same starts and goals. <a href="assets/goals/display_manifest.json">Counts, identities and visual provenance ↗</a></figcaption><details class="goal-rgb-details"><summary>Inspect unmodified RGB execution frames</summary><div><label>Fixed episode<select id="goal-rgb-episode"><option value="0">0</option><option value="1">1</option><option value="2">2</option></select></label><div class="goal-path-scroll" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="Unmodified RGB goal-control execution frames"><img id="goal-rgb-image" src="assets/goals/goal_rgb_checkpoint0_episode0.svg" alt="Unmodified RGB frames for random actions, JEPA H3, and the privileged state reference, episode zero and model seed zero. Each row ends at its first hit, death or action limit."></div></div></details></figure></div></details></section>
<section class="article-section case-section" id="state"><div class="section-label">02 / THE REPRESENTATION CAN FAIL</div><div class="prose"><h2>Add stochastic motion.<br>The state readout breaks.</h2><p>Now add hidden wind while keeping the fresh gray background. With the same grid architecture and training schedule, the held-out position readout rises from subpixel error to about <strong>21 pixels</strong>. The failure repeats across three model fits. Keeping the gray background fixed within each episode instead brings the error back to 0.67 pixels under wind.</p><p>The image makes the failure visible; the readout supplies its quantitative support. A missing cube in a decoded image alone would not establish that its position was absent from the latent.</p></div>'''
html+=viewer('gray_frame_wind','Fresh visual noise. Hidden wind.','The red cube and native noise are unchanged from the saved evaluation. These are model outputs, not illustrations generated to resemble a failure. The real-future reconstruction export is still pending in this internal draft.')
html+='''<div class="two-number-result"><div><span>Fresh noise · still</span><strong>0.12 <small>px</small></strong></div><div><span>Fresh noise · wind</span><strong>21.33 <small>px</small></strong></div><p>Mean position-readout L₁ error · three model fits per condition · unchanged 512-dimensional grid</p></div><div class="prose"><p>Jointly learning what to encode creates a second problem besides predicting it. A feature can be easy to predict and irrelevant to the task. Non-collapse constrains the latent’s overall distribution; it does not decide which state deserves space.</p><p>An episode-static Gaussian nuisance gives the simplest analytical example: encode that nuisance and copy it forward. The marginal remains noncollapsed and prediction is exact, even though the controlled state is ignored. This known shortcut motivates the question, but it does not explain the high-loss wind run above by itself.</p></div><details class="quiet-details"><summary>The static-nuisance argument, in one equation</summary><div><p>If an observable nuisance b is independent of state and distributed as N(0, I), an encoder zₜ = b and predictor ẑₜ₊₁ = zₜ have zero population prediction error and a Gaussian latent marginal. This extends the static-background construction of <a href="https://arxiv.org/html/2211.10831v1">Sobal et al. (2022)</a> to the stated marginal constraint. It assumes that nuisance recovery and Gaussianization are realizable; a finite texture does not literally satisfy those assumptions.</p><p>The empirical gray-frame/wind condition has high prediction loss. Its failed readouts are not evidence that training reached this zero-loss construction.</p></div></details></section>
<section class="article-section case-section" id="planning"><div class="section-label">03 / EVEN A PERFECT MEAN CAN MISLEAD PLANNING</div><div class="prose"><h2>Knowing the mean future<br>is not enough to choose.</h2><p>Suppose the representation keeps the state and the predictor is perfect. For a fixed representation, an unrestricted predictor minimizing population squared error returns the conditional mean. A planner that evaluates only that mean can still choose the wrong action.</p><p>Take a goal at zero. Action A reaches −1 or +1 with equal probability. Action B reaches 0.1. A’s mean is exactly the goal, even though neither of its possible outcomes is.</p></div>
<figure class="mean-demo" aria-labelledby="mean-title"><div class="figure-head"><div><span class="figure-kicker">ANALYTICAL EXAMPLE · EXACT DISCRETE OUTCOMES</span><h3 id="mean-title">The mean favors the wrong action.</h3></div><div class="score-toggle" role="group" aria-label="Choose how to score the two actions"><button type="button" data-score="mean" aria-pressed="true">Score the mean</button><button type="button" data-score="outcomes" aria-pressed="false">Score the outcomes</button></div></div><svg class="outcome-axis" viewBox="0 0 820 180" role="img" aria-labelledby="outcome-title outcome-desc"><title id="outcome-title">Action A has outcomes minus one and plus one; action B has outcome zero point one</title><desc id="outcome-desc">The goal is zero. Action A's conditional mean is zero, but each outcome has squared goal distance one. Action B has squared goal distance zero point zero one.</desc><path class="outcome-guide" d="M80 105H740"/><path class="outcome-goal" d="M410 32V141"/><g class="outcome-ticks"><text x="80" y="135" text-anchor="middle">−1</text><text x="410" y="158" text-anchor="middle">Goal 0</text><text x="443" y="135" text-anchor="middle">0.1</text><text x="740" y="135" text-anchor="middle">+1</text></g><path class="outcome-a-link" d="M80 105Q245 33 410 105Q575 33 740 105"/><circle class="outcome-a" cx="80" cy="105" r="9"/><circle class="outcome-a" cx="740" cy="105" r="9"/><circle class="outcome-a-mean" cx="410" cy="105" r="9"/><rect class="outcome-b" x="436" y="98" width="14" height="14"/><g class="outcome-labels"><text x="80" y="77" text-anchor="middle">A · 50%</text><text x="740" y="77" text-anchor="middle">A · 50%</text><text x="410" y="27" text-anchor="middle">mean of A</text><text x="486" y="81" text-anchor="middle">B · certain</text></g></svg><div class="action-scores"><div class="action-a"><span>Action A</span><p data-equation-a>0² = <strong>0</strong></p></div><div class="action-b"><span>Action B</span><p data-equation-b>0.1² = <strong>0.01</strong></p></div></div><p class="choice-line" data-choice aria-live="polite"><strong>Scoring only the mean chooses A.</strong> Its predicted mean lies at the goal.</p><figcaption>These are exact analytical costs, not scores from either trained world model. The same expected squared goal cost selects B. No literal mixture of decoded images is assumed: the decoder can be nonlinear.</figcaption></figure>
<div class="prose"><p>The missing term is variance. For a fixed goal and a square-integrable next latent, expected squared distance decomposes as:</p></div><div class="short-equation"><math display="block" aria-label="Expected squared goal distance equals squared distance of the mean plus the trace of conditional covariance"><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><msup><mrow><mo>‖</mo><msup><mi>Z</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mi>𝔼</mi><msup><mi>Z</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mtext>tr Cov</mtext><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>Z</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></math><p>All quantities are conditional on the same context and action. A variance term shared by every action adds a constant; differing variance can change the ranking.</p></div>
<div class="prose"><h3>Recursing a mean adds another error.</h3><p>Let xₜ₊₁ = xₜ² + ξₜ, where each ξ is independently −1 or +1 with equal probability. From x₀ = 0, the first state has mean zero. But after two steps the actual mean is one. Repeatedly applying the perfect one-step mean map g(x) = x² stays at zero.</p></div>
<figure class="recursion-figure"><span class="figure-kicker">ANALYTICAL EXAMPLE · xₜ₊₁ = xₜ² + ξₜ, ξₜ ∈ {−1, +1}</span><div class="recursion-grid"><span></span><span>Start</span><span>After one step</span><span>After two steps</span><strong>Actual outcomes</strong><div>0</div><div>−1 or +1</div><div>0 or 2<small>mean = 1</small></div><strong>Roll the mean forward</strong><div>0</div><div>0</div><div>0<small>predicted = 0</small></div></div><figcaption>The one-step conditional mean is exact. The mismatch comes from applying a nonlinear transition to a mean instead of propagating the distribution. This is a standard analytical counterexample, not a diagnosis of a particular JEPA checkpoint.</figcaption></figure>
<div class="prose"><h3>The empirical planning case is still being tested.</h3><p>A persistent gray background with wind is a useful candidate: position remains accessible in the frozen representation, while historical JEPA and flow control results differ. The old controllers also differ, so those results do not establish that mean prediction caused the gap.</p></div><div class="draft-banner" data-internal-note><strong>Internal evidence gate.</strong> Insert the completed shared-CNN comparison and actual agent trajectories here. Preserve failed controls. If the comparison does not support a stochastic-planning failure, do not present it as one.</div>'''
html+=viewer('gray_episode_wind','Persistent visual noise. Hidden wind.','This historical prediction example is a candidate for the empirical planning study. It is not a policy-execution comparison and does not establish the cause of a control failure.')
html+='''<div class="agent-trajectory-slot" data-internal-note><span class="figure-kicker">ACTUAL AGENT EXECUTIONS · PENDING</span><p>Real environment trajectories from the final JEPA and flow policies belong here. Recorded-action model forecasts will not be substituted for policy behavior.</p></div></section>
<section class="article-section article-ending"><div class="prose"><h2>Abstraction needs a decision criterion.</h2><p>The successful case shows why predicting embeddings is attractive: visual noise can be discarded while useful state survives. The failed readout shows that this does not happen automatically. Separately, the exact planning examples show that even a perfect conditional mean can be insufficient for a stochastic decision.</p><p>The empirical question is where those problems occur in a fitted model. A representation test, a model forecast and a real action sequence provide different evidence. The final claim must follow all three.</p></div></section>
<section class="article-section methods-section" id="methods"><details class="quiet-details"><summary>How these examples were measured</summary><div><h3>Model and data scope</h3><p>The historical grid JEPA uses a 4×4×32 latent (512 coordinates), 500,000 uniform-random transitions in each gray-noise condition and 8,500 training updates. Three initialization/data-order seeds share each condition’s fixed dataset. Flow is a separate reconstruction-trained recipe; differences in architecture, supervision and inference prevent treating it as an objective-only ablation.</p><h3>Position readout</h3><p>The two headline numbers are mean held-out position L₁ error, |x error| + |y error|, across three grid model fits: 0.115 ± 0.012 pixels for gray_frame_still and 21.330 ± 0.057 for gray_frame_wind. These are errors of the fitted readout, not an information-theoretic claim. The failing wind condition has high prediction loss. Probe supervision and decoder fitting are outside world-model training.</p><h3>Visual provenance</h3><p>Fresh frame packets contain eight observed context frames and sixteen matched future frames from frozen checkpoints. Their first three eligible windows were fixed without inspecting model outputs, and require no reset across the context and future. This selects surviving illustrations. The fresh flow checkpoint is the corrected PPO-training recipe, separate from the earlier red-cube benchmark flow fit. These visualization samples use eight function evaluations; visualization sampling and policy-training protocols are recorded separately. Every fresh viewer links its exact frame, checkpoint and data hashes. Any historical fallback is explicitly labeled; its travel-based scene selection and inherited GIF palette are recorded in the <a href="assets/historical/manifest.json">historical manifest</a>.</p><p>All policy claims use real environment evaluation. Still-world survival alone does not certify visual feedback, because alternating state-blind actions can survive. Flow sampling and mean-only planning are not synonymous with complete learned-controller recipes.</p><h3>Exact variance check</h3><p>Enumerating the true nine-branch wind law for two fixed encoders changes the minimum expected latent-goal-cost action in 2.65% of safe black-background cases and 1.08% of matched static-gray cases; regrets are small and no physical-control improvement is established. The mean plus a scalar variance term suffices for this objective. <a href="../../research/oracle_variance_results.md">Exact diagnostic and controls ↗</a></p><h3>Related work</h3><p><a href="https://arxiv.org/html/2603.19312v3">LeWorldModel</a> learns an encoder and next-latent predictor jointly with Gaussian regularization. <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.29059">FlowWM</a> models stochastic futures in frozen pretrained features and evaluates future perception and mode coverage. Our small flow reference is a separate recipe; neither citation supplies the red-cube control evidence.</p><h3>Sources and complete evidence</h3><p><a href="../../research/redcube_audit.md">Red-cube evidence audit</a> · <a href="../../research/redcube_evidence.json">Full historical measurements</a> · <a href="../v1/main.pdf">Archived V1 paper</a> · <a href="../html/index.html">Archived V1 companion</a> · <a href="../html-v2/index.html">Archived V2 working companion</a></p><p><a href="https://arxiv.org/html/2603.19312v3">LeWorldModel</a> supplies the direct joint-prediction recipe. <a href="https://arxiv.org/html/2511.08544v3">LeJEPA</a> introduces SIGReg. <a href="https://arxiv.org/html/2211.10831v1">Sobal et al.</a> study the static-nuisance shortcut, and <a href="https://arxiv.org/html/2512.24497v4">Terver et al.</a> discuss conditional means in multimodal planning. The standard conditional-expectation and variance identities above are background, not new theorem claims. The conditional-mean result concerns direct next-state regression. A flow model can fit a velocity field with squared error while its final samples represent a distribution.</p></div></details></section></main><footer><span>STATE · FUTURES · DECISIONS</span><a href="#main">Back to the beginning ↑</a></footer></body></html>'''
(BASE/'index.html').write_text(html)
print('Wrote',BASE/'index.html')
